--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  In an event-driven system, the producer and consumer are deployed
  independently. The moment a producer adds a field, renames one, or changes a
  type, every downstream consumer is at risk - and with plain JSON, you find out
  in production.

  This project shows the alternative: the schema IS the contract, enforced at
  serialization time and version-checked by a registry before a bad schema ever
  reaches a broker.

  Concretely, it demonstrates how to:

    - Enforce a typed data contract between services using Avro and Confluent
      Schema Registry
    - Evolve that contract without redeploying every consumer in lockstep
    - Keep schema registration out of the application and inside CI/CD, where
      it belongs
    - Persist consumed events transactionally to PostgreSQL via Spring Data JPA
    - Run the entire stack (Kafka, Schema Registry, Postgres) locally in one
      command


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  2. ARCHITECTURE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

     HTTP POST /users
           |
           v
  +---------------------+        +----------------------+
  |   producer-app      |------->|   Schema Registry    |
  |   :8080             |   1    |   :8081              |
  |                     |<-------|  (compatibility gate)|
  |  REST -> validate   |        +----------------------+
  |  -> map to Avro     |
  +----------+----------+
             | 2  Avro-serialized record
             v
     +-------------------+
     |   Kafka broker    |   topic: users.v1
     |   :29092          |
     +---------+---------+
               | 3  schema id resolved from registry
               v
  +---------------------+
  |   consumer-app      |
  |   :8089             |
  |                     |
  |  deserialize Avro   |
  |  -> map to JPA      |
  +----------+----------+
             | 4
             v
     +-------------------+
     |   PostgreSQL      |   users.contact
     |   :5432           |
     +-------------------+

  THE FLOW, STEP BY STEP

  1. producer-app accepts a UserCreateRequest, applies Bean Validation, and
     maps it to a generated Avro User record via MapStruct.

  2. The Avro serializer resolves the schema against the registry and writes a
     SCHEMA ID into the message header - the payload itself carries no schema,
     keeping records compact.

  3. consumer-app reads the schema ID off the record, fetches the writer schema
     from the registry, and deserializes against its own reader schema. Avro
     reconciles the two.

  4. The record is mapped to a UserEntity and persisted to users.contact.

  The producer and consumer share NO code except the common-schemas module -
  which contains only the .avsc definition, not business logic. That is the
  point: the contract is data, not a shared jar of DTOs.


