  Local infra      Docker Compose                      -


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  4. PROJECT LAYOUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  kafka-schema-registry-spring-demo/
  |
  +-- common-schemas/        Avro .avsc definitions -> generated Java classes
  |   +-- src/main/avro/
  |       +-- User.avsc      the data contract
  |
  +-- producer-app/          REST endpoint -> Avro -> Kafka         (:8080)
  +-- consumer-app/          Kafka -> Avro -> JPA -> PostgreSQL     (:8089)
  +-- postman/               ready-to-import request collection
  +-- docker-compose.yml     Kafka, Zookeeper, Schema Registry, Postgres
  +-- pom.xml                Maven aggregator

  common-schemas is built first and installed to the local Maven repo. Both apps
  depend on the generated classes - so a schema change is a COMPILE-TIME break,
  not a runtime surprise.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  5. QUICKSTART
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  PREREQUISITES
    JDK 21+, Maven 3.9+ (or the bundled mvnw), Docker Desktop with ~4 GB
    allocated.


  STEP 1 - START THE INFRASTRUCTURE

    docker compose up -d

  Wait for Schema Registry to report healthy - it depends on Kafka and takes
  about 30 seconds:

    curl -s http://localhost:8081/subjects

  An empty array [] means it is up and no schemas are registered yet.

    Service            Endpoint                   Credentials
    -----------------  -------------------------  ---------------------
    Kafka broker       localhost:29092            -
    Schema Registry    http://localhost:8081      -
    PostgreSQL         localhost:5432             kafka / kafkaConfluent


  STEP 2 - BUILD (GENERATE THE AVRO CLASSES FIRST)

    ./mvnw clean install -DskipTests

  *** THIS STEP IS NOT OPTIONAL ***
  common-schemas runs the Avro Maven plugin to generate User.java from
  User.avsc. Skip it and both apps fail to compile with:
      cannot find symbol: class User


  STEP 3 - CREATE THE DATABASE SCHEMA

    docker compose exec -T postgres psql -U kafka -d kafka < ddl/schema.sql

  DDL reference:

    CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS users;

    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users.contact (
        id          int8 GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
        userid      text,
        email       text,
        phone       text,
        first_name  text,
        last_name   text,
        is_active   bool NOT NULL DEFAULT true,
        created_at  timestamptz,
        age         int4
    );

    CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_users_created_at
        ON users.contact (created_at DESC);
    CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_users_email
        ON users.contact (email);
    CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_users_is_active
        ON users.contact (is_active);

  The three indexes cover the common access patterns: recent-first listing,
  lookup by email, and filtering active users.


  STEP 4 - RUN BOTH SERVICES

  Two terminals.

    # terminal 1 - consumer (start first so no messages are missed)
    cd consumer-app && ../mvnw spring-boot:run

    # terminal 2 - producer
    cd producer-app && ../mvnw spring-boot:run


  STEP 5 - SEND AN EVENT

    curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/users \
      -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
      -d '{
        "id": "u-1001",
        "email": "ada@example.com",
        "phone": "5551234567",
        "firstName": "Ada",
        "lastName": "Lovelace",
        "isActive": true,
        "age": 36
      }'


  STEP 6 - VERIFY END TO END

    # the schema auto-registered on first publish
    curl -s http://localhost:8081/subjects
    -> ["users.v1-value"]

    # the row landed
    docker compose exec -T postgres psql -U kafka -d kafka \
      -c "SELECT userid, email, first_name, age FROM users.contact;"

     userid  |      email       | first_name | age
    ---------+------------------+------------+-----
     u-1001  | ada@example.com  | Ada        |  36


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  6. HOW IT WORKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  THE PRODUCER SIDE

  The REST layer never touches Avro. A UserCreateRequest DTO carries Bean
  Validation annotations; MapStruct generates the mapper to the Avro User
  record at compile time (-Amapstruct.defaultComponentModel=spring, so mappers
  are injectable Spring beans).

  KafkaAvroSerializer then does the interesting part:

    1. Extracts the writer schema from the User record
    2. Looks up (or registers) that schema under subject users.v1-value
    3. Prefixes the payload with a magic byte and the 4-byte schema ID
    4. Serializes the record body in Avro binary - NO FIELD NAMES ON THE WIRE

  That last point is why Avro beats JSON at volume: field names live in the
  registry, not in every message.


  THE CONSUMER SIDE

  KafkaAvroDeserializer reads the schema ID from the payload prefix, fetches the
  writer schema from the registry (cached after first fetch), and deserializes
  using Avro's schema resolution - reconciling the writer schema against the
  consumer's compiled-in reader schema.

  Persistence goes through a @Transactional service so a mapping or constraint
  failure rolls back cleanly rather than leaving a half-written row.


  WHERE COMPATIBILITY IS ENFORCED

  Compatibility is checked WHEN A SCHEMA IS REGISTERED, not when a message is
  consumed. Under BACKWARD compatibility, the registry rejects a new version
  that a current consumer could not read - so an incompatible change fails at
  registration time, in CI, rather than as a deserialization storm in
  production.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  7. CONFIGURATION REFERENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  PRODUCER - producer-app/src/main/resources/application.yml

    Property                                       Default
    ---------------------------------------------  ----------------------------
    server.port                                    8080
    spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers                 localhost:29092
    spring.kafka.producer.key-serializer           StringSerializer
    spring.kafka.producer.value-serializer         KafkaAvroSerializer
    spring.kafka.properties.schema.registry.url    http://localhost:8081
    spring.kafka.properties.auto.register.schemas  true (local) / false (cloud)
    app.kafka.topic                                users.v1


  CONSUMER - consumer-app/src/main/resources/application.yml

    Property                                       Default
    ---------------------------------------------  ----------------------------
    server.port                                    8089
    spring.kafka.consumer.group-id                 users-consumer
    spring.kafka.consumer.auto-offset-reset        earliest
    spring.kafka.properties.specific.avro.reader   true
    spring.datasource.url                          jdbc:postgresql://
                                                     localhost:5432/kafka
    spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto                  validate

  NOTE: ddl-auto: validate is deliberate. "update" lets Hibernate silently
  reshape production tables; "validate" refuses to start when the entity and
  the table disagree.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  8. SCHEMA EVOLUTION WALKTHROUGH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Adding a field without breaking the running consumer.


  STEP 1 - SET THE COMPATIBILITY MODE

    curl -X PUT http://localhost:8081/config/users.v1-value \
      -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
      -d '{"compatibility": "BACKWARD"}'


  STEP 2 - ADD AN OPTIONAL FIELD, WITH A DEFAULT

    {
      "name": "nickname",
      "type": ["null", "string"],
      "default": null
    }

  The "default" is what makes this backward compatible. Without it, an old
  consumer hits a record missing the field and has nothing to fall back on -
  the registry will reject the schema.


  STEP 3 - REGENERATE AND VERIFY

    ./mvnw -pl common-schemas clean install

  Dry-run the compatibility check before you deploy anything:

    curl -X POST \
      http://localhost:8081/compatibility/subjects/users.v1-value/versions/latest \
      -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
      -d '{"schema": "<escaped new schema>"}'
    -> {"is_compatible": true}


  STEP 4 - ROLL THE PRODUCER FIRST

  Deploy the producer with the new schema. The OLD CONSUMER KEEPS RUNNING - it
  reads new records and simply ignores the unknown field. Upgrade the consumer
  whenever convenient.


  COMPATIBILITY MODES AT A GLANCE

    Mode       Safe change                          Upgrade order
    ---------  -----------------------------------  ------------------------
    BACKWARD   Add optional field, delete field     Consumers, then producers
    FORWARD    Add field, delete optional field     Producers, then consumers
    FULL       Add/delete optional fields only      Either order
    NONE       Anything                             You are on your own


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  9. RUNNING AGAINST CONFLUENT CLOUD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Provision a cluster and Schema Registry at https://confluent.cloud/, then
  export:

    export CLOUD_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS="pkc-xxxxx.us-central1.gcp.confluent.cloud:9092"
    export CLOUD_API_KEY="<kafka-api-key>"
    export CLOUD_API_SECRET="<kafka-api-secret>"
    export SR_URL="https://psrc-xxxxx.us-central1.gcp.confluent.cloud"
    export SR_API_KEY="<registry-api-key>"
    export SR_API_SECRET="<registry-api-secret>"

  Then:

    cd consumer-app && ../mvnw spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.profiles=cloud
    cd producer-app && ../mvnw spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.profiles=cloud

  The cloud profile connects over SASL_SSL and flips two settings that matter:

    auto.register.schemas: false   # apps may not mutate the registry
    use.latest.version: true       # serialize against the registered schema

  WHY: in a shared environment, a mis-deployed service that can auto-register
  schemas can poison the contract for every other consumer. Registration
  becomes a deliberate CI/CD step with review, not a side effect of a pod
  starting up.

  *** Never commit real API keys. .env and *.local.yml are gitignored. ***


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  10. TESTING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    ./mvnw test                     unit tests
    ./mvnw verify                   + integration tests
    ./mvnw -pl producer-app test    single module

  UNIT TESTS cover the MapStruct mappers, the validation layer, and the
  persistence service with MockProducer / mocked repositories - no broker
  required.

  INTEGRATION TESTS use Testcontainers (https://www.testcontainers.org/) to
  spin up real Kafka, Schema Registry, and PostgreSQL containers per run. This
  is the only way to meaningfully test schema resolution: an embedded broker
  without a registry exercises none of the code paths that actually matter
  here.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  11. TROUBLESHOOTING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  > Subject not found; error code: 40401

    The consumer started before any schema was registered. Publish one event
    from the producer first, or pre-register the schema:

      curl -X POST http://localhost:8081/subjects/users.v1-value/versions \
        -H "Content-Type: application/vnd.schemaregistry.v1+json" \
        -d '{"schema": "<escaped User.avsc>"}'


  > cannot find symbol: class User

    common-schemas was not installed. Run the following from the PROJECT ROOT,
    not from inside a module directory:

      ./mvnw clean install -DskipTests


  > Consumer receives nothing

    Check the consumer group's offset position:

      docker compose exec kafka kafka-consumer-groups \
        --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --describe --group users-consumer

    LAG = 0 with no records means the group already consumed them. Reset:

      docker compose exec kafka kafka-consumer-groups \
        --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --group users-consumer \
        --reset-offsets --to-earliest --topic users.v1 --execute


  > Schema being registered under an unexpected subject

    Confluent's default TopicNameStrategy derives the subject from the topic
    (users.v1-value). If you are publishing multiple event types to one topic
    you want RecordNameStrategy instead:

      spring.kafka.properties.value.subject.name.strategy: \
        io.confluent.kafka.serializers.subject.RecordNameStrategy


  > Schema Registry container will not start

    It depends on Kafka being fully up. "docker compose logs schema-registry"
    usually shows connection retries. Restart just that service after Kafka
    settles:

      docker compose restart schema-registry


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  12. DESIGN DECISIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  AVRO OVER JSON SCHEMA OR PROTOBUF
    Avro's compact binary encoding and mature schema-resolution rules make it
    the default in the Kafka ecosystem, and Confluent's tooling is deepest
    here. Protobuf is a reasonable swap - the registry supports it and the
    serializer config is a one-line change.

  MAPSTRUCT OVER HAND-WRITTEN MAPPERS
    Compile-time generation means a schema field change surfaces as a build
    failure rather than a silently null column.

  auto.register.schemas=false OUTSIDE LOCAL
    Schema registration is a deployment concern with review and rollback, not
    something an application does implicitly on startup.

  SEPARATE PRODUCER AND CONSUMER MODULES
    They share only the schema. That is the constraint that makes the
    schema-evolution demo real rather than theoretical - if they shared a DTO
    jar, evolution would just be a recompile.

  ddl-auto: validate
    Fail loudly at startup on entity/table drift instead of letting Hibernate
    reshape a table underneath you.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  13. ROADMAP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. Dead-letter topic for deserialization failures
  2. Testcontainers integration test suite
  3. GitHub Actions workflow - build, test, registry compatibility check on PR
  4. Idempotent consumer with a processed-event ledger
  5. Micrometer metrics + Grafana dashboard for consumer lag
  6. Protobuf variant on a parallel branch




